# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction {#introduction}"
msgstr "Panimula {#introduction}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You have probably heard of the uncertainty principle, even outside of your physics courses. A common colloquial restatement of uncertainty is \"By looking at something, you influence it.\" That is certainly true. But a more physical way of describing uncertainty is that there are certain physical observables that have an incompatibility that prevents them both from being simultaneously known to arbitrary accuracy. Many students first encounter the pair of incompatible variables $x$ and $p_x$, meaning the position along one axis called the $x$-axis, and the linear momentum along that direction, respectively. For those variables the constraint on uncertainty is written\n"
"$$\\Delta x \\Delta p_x \\geq \\frac{\\hbar}{2}.$$\n"
"Here, $\\Delta x$ is called the \"uncertainty in $x$\", which has the same definition as standard deviation in statistics, and can be defined as\n"
"$$\\Delta x = \\sqrt{\\langle x^2 \\rangle - \\langle x \\rangle^2}.$$\n"
"$\\Delta p_x$ is defined in the same way.\n"
"Here, we will not derive this uncertainty relation; we will point out that it is consistent with our understanding of classical waves. That is, a wave with truly one perfect frequency $f$ and wavelength $\\lambda$ would go on and on forever as a perfect sinusoid. Quantum mechanically, this would correspond to knowing the momentum perfectly according to de Broglie's hypothesis: $\\lambda = h/p$. But in order to know $where$ a wave-like particle is located, the wave describing it must become more sharply peaked in space, like a very narrow Gaussian, for example. We know that we can express any continuous function, including such sharply-peaked wave functions, as a Fourier series of sinusoidal functions with different wavelengths. But as the wave function becomes more sharply peaked (and the position is better known), we will require more terms in the Fourier series, meaning a mixture of more wavelengths (and thus, quantum mechanically, more values of momentum).\n"
msgstr ""
"Malamang narinig mo na ang uncertainty principle, kahit sa labas ng iyong mga kurso sa physics. Isang karaniwang kolokyal na paglalahad ng uncertainty ay \"Sa pagtingin sa isang bagay, naiimpluwensyahan mo ito.\" Iyon ay tama. Ngunit ang mas physical na paraan ng paglalarawan sa uncertainty ay may ilang physical observable na hindi tugma sa isa't isa na pumipigil sa dalawa nito na malaman nang sabay sa arbitrary na katumpakan. Maraming estudyante ang unang nakakaengkwentro sa pares ng hindi tugmang variables na $x$ at $p_x$, ibig sabihin ang posisyon sa kahabaan ng isang axis na tinatawag na $x$-axis, at ang linear momentum sa kahabaan ng direksyon na iyon, ayon sa pagkakabanggit. Para sa mga variable na iyon ang hadlang sa uncertainty ay isinusulat bilang\n"
"$$\\Delta x \\Delta p_x \\geq \\frac{\\hbar}{2}.$$\n"
"Dito, ang $\\Delta x$ ay tinatawag na \"uncertainty sa $x$\", na may parehong depinisyon gaya ng standard deviation sa statistics, at maaaring tukuyin bilang\n"
"$$\\Delta x = \\sqrt{\\langle x^2 \\rangle - \\langle x \\rangle^2}.$$\n"
"Ang $\\Delta p_x$ ay tinutukoy sa parehong paraan.\n"
"Dito, hindi natin i-de-derive ang uncertainty relation na ito; ituturo natin na ito ay tugma sa ating pag-unawa sa classical waves. Ibig sabihin, ang alon na may talagang isang perpektong frequency na $f$ at wavelength na $\\lambda$ ay magpapatuloy nang walang hanggan bilang isang perpektong sinusoid. Sa quantum mechanically, ito ay katumbas ng pagkakaalam nang perpekto sa momentum ayon sa hypothesis ni de Broglie: $\\lambda = h/p$. Ngunit upang malaman kung $saan$ matatagpuan ang isang wave-like particle, ang alon na naglalarawan nito ay dapat maging mas matalas na peaked sa espasyo, tulad ng isang napakakitid na Gaussian, halimbawa. Alam natin na maaari nating ipahayag ang anumang tuloy-tuloy na function, kabilang ang ganoong matatalim na peaked wave functions, bilang isang Fourier series ng mga sinusoidal functions na may iba't ibang wavelength. Ngunit habang nagiging mas matalim ang peak ng wave function (at mas nalalaman ang posisyon), kakailanganin natin ng mas maraming termino sa Fourier series, ibig sabihin ay pinaghalong maraming wavelength (at sa gayon, quantum mechanically, mas maraming halaga ng momentum).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Stated more simply: a state with a well-defined momentum (a perfect sinusoid in space) has very uncertain position. A state with a well-defined position (like a Dirac delta distribution) has a very uncertain momentum.\n"
msgstr "Sa mas simpleng pagsasabi: ang isang state na may malinaw na tinukoy na momentum (isang perpektong sinusoid sa espasyo) ay may napakawalang katiyakang posisyon. Ang isang state na may malinaw na tinukoy na posisyon (tulad ng isang Dirac delta distribution) ay may napakawalang katiyakang momentum.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There are other variables that exhibit such incompatibility. For example, a particle's spin may have a well-defined projection along one axis, but then we know nothing about the project on an orthogonal axis. For example the state $|0\\rangle \\sim |\\uparrow\\rangle$ (for a qubit or spin-1/2 particle) has a definite projection along the $z$ axis (of 1 in the context of a qubit, and of $\\hbar/2$ in the context of a spin-1/2 particle). But this state can be written as a superposition of two states each of which has a well-defined projection onto the $x$ axis:\n"
"$$|0\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|+\\rangle_x+|-\\rangle_x)$$\n"
"or equivalently\n"
"$$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix}\\right].$$\n"
"$|+\\rangle_x$ has a well-defined projection onto $x$, as does $|-\\rangle_x$. So if we specify the projection of a state along the $x$ axis, we do not know the projection along the $z$ axis. And if we specify the projection on the $z$ axis, we don't know the projection along $x$. There are minor differences when discussing this in the context of spin and in qubits. But generally speaking, eigenstates of the Pauli matrices have an interesting relationship that we can explore.\n"
"Throughout this lesson, we will be experimentally checking our intuition for the uncertainty in these incompatible variables, and verifying that uncertainty relations hold on IBM&reg; quantum computers.\n"
msgstr ""
"May iba pang mga variable na nagpapakita ng ganitong hindi pagkakatugma. Halimbawa, ang spin ng isang particle ay maaaring may malinaw na tinukoy na projection sa kahabaan ng isang axis, ngunit wala tayong alam tungkol sa project sa isang orthogonal axis. Halimbawa ang state na $|0\\rangle \\sim |\\uparrow\\rangle$ (para sa isang qubit o spin-1/2 particle) ay may tiyak na projection sa kahabaan ng $z$ axis (ng 1 sa konteksto ng isang qubit, at ng $\\hbar/2$ sa konteksto ng isang spin-1/2 particle). Ngunit ang state na ito ay maaaring isulat bilang superposition ng dalawang state na bawat isa ay may malinaw na tinukoy na projection sa $x$ axis:\n"
"$$|0\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|+\\rangle_x+|-\\rangle_x)$$\n"
"o katumbas\n"
"$$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0\\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1\\end{pmatrix}\\right].$$\n"
"Ang $|+\\rangle_x$ ay may malinaw na tinukoy na projection sa $x$, tulad din ng $|-\\rangle_x$. Kaya kung tutukuyin natin ang projection ng isang state sa kahabaan ng $x$ axis, hindi natin alam ang projection sa kahabaan ng $z$ axis. At kung tutukuyin natin ang projection sa $z$ axis, hindi natin alam ang projection sa kahabaan ng $x$. May mga maliliit na pagkakaiba kapag pinag-uusapan ito sa konteksto ng spin at ng mga qubit. Ngunit sa pangkalahatan, ang mga eigenstate ng Pauli matrices ay may kawili-wiling relasyon na maaari nating tuklasin.\n"
"Sa buong lesson na ito, susuriin natin nang eksperimental ang ating intuwisyon para sa uncertainty sa mga hindi tugmang variable na ito, at beberipikahin na ang mga uncertainty relation ay totoo sa mga IBM&reg; quantum computer.\n"

